# The capture writes one sheet per shot in whatever spec it was handed, and a
# manifest beside them. Three sheets are committed as the baseline a candidate
# layout is held against; README.md says which three and why.
#
# Everything else must not be committed, and that cannot be left to whoever runs
# the capture next. The fourth working agreement is explicit about it: "if a
# generated file must **not** be committed, that has to be arranged by
# construction -- an ignore rule -- and not by remembering."
#
# A candidate layout is a scratch class staged into the project for one run, so
# the sheets it produces are scratch too. Without this file they sit untracked
# in the tree and the build gate's last step -- the one that fails a push whose
# run rewrote the repository -- goes red for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the push.
#
# The three negations are not load-bearing today, because an ignore rule cannot
# govern a file git already tracks. They are here so that re-adding a sheet
# after a deliberate deletion works without anybody having to remember this file
# exists.
*.png
manifest.json
!as-built-build.png
!as-built-placed.png
!as-built-offer.png
!chosen-build-phase.png

# overflows/ is the exception, and it is one on purpose: the sheets there are
# the shipped chrome at the two late-round states the roster's growth broke it
# at, first rendered as candidates by issue #282 and redrawn as what ships once
# #285 signed. They are committed beside the spec that draws them, the way
# docs/frames/ commits its frames, because a late round is not a state the
# baseline sheets above can show.
!overflows/*.png
!overflows/manifest.json
